30 Days of Ansible – Day 19/30

🔐 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗩𝗮𝘂𝗹𝘁 – 𝗦𝗲𝗰𝘂𝗿𝗲 𝗬𝗼𝘂𝗿 𝗦𝗲𝗰𝗿𝗲𝘁𝘀

Ansible automation often needs to handle:

🔑 Database passwords
🔐 API keys
🎫 Access tokens
☁️ Cloud credentials

Would you store them directly in your playbook?

❌ 𝗡𝗲𝘃𝗲𝗿.

This is where Ansible Vault helps.

🧩 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗶𝘀 𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗩𝗮𝘂𝗹𝘁?

Ansible Vault encrypts sensitive data so secrets aren't stored as plain text.

🔐 𝗖𝗿𝗲𝗮𝘁𝗲 𝗮 𝗩𝗮𝘂𝗹𝘁 𝗙𝗶𝗹𝗲

ansible-vault create secrets.yml

Useful commands:

ansible-vault view secrets.yml
ansible-vault edit secrets.yml
ansible-vault encrypt secrets.yml
ansible-vault decrypt secrets.yml

Run a playbook with:

ansible-playbook site.yml --ask-vault-pass

🏢 𝗥𝗲𝗮𝗹-𝗪𝗼𝗿𝗹𝗱 𝗘𝘅𝗮𝗺𝗽𝗹𝗲

Instead of:

❌ Plaintext passwords in Git
❌ Hardcoded API keys
❌ Exposed credentials

Use:

Git → 🔐 Encrypted secrets → Ansible → Servers

⚠️ 𝗕𝗲𝘀𝘁 𝗣𝗿𝗮𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗰𝗲𝘀

✅ Encrypt sensitive variables
✅ Protect Vault passwords
✅ Never commit plaintext secrets
✅ Avoid printing secrets with debug
✅ Use no_log: true for sensitive tasks

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗧𝗮𝗸𝗲𝗮𝘄𝗮𝘆

Automation is powerful, but security must come first.

𝗔𝗻𝘀𝗶𝗯𝗹𝗲 𝗩𝗮𝘂𝗹𝘁 = 𝗦𝗲𝗰𝘂𝗿𝗲 𝗦𝗲𝗰𝗿𝗲𝘁𝘀 + 𝗦𝗮𝗳𝗲𝗿 𝗔𝘂𝘁𝗼𝗺𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 🔐🚀
